## Problem-size knobs for the reference scripts.
##
## Each record declares one (script, profile) pair and the literal assignments
## substituted to bring the script to a size that completes on a workstation.
## The substitution is applied by tools/reduce.R, which asserts that
##
##   (a) each knob name matches EXACTLY ONE top-level assignment line,
##   (b) the number of lines that differ from the reference equals the number
##       of knobs, and no other line differs,
##   (c) every changed line is a bare `<name> = <literal>;` on both sides,
##
## so no substitution can reach a line that carries algebra. Line NUMBERING is
## preserved (one line in, one line out, no inserted header) because the
## extraction manifest addresses the reference by line range.
##
## Why this exists: at the published settings the dyad script allocates
## RE_metric as 20601 x 20601 (3.40 GB) and three online-smoother cell
## triangles at N = 30001 (~10.8 GB), then scans 513 epsilon x 20601 j over
## rows up to 20601 long. That does not complete on a 32 GB workstation in a
## usable time, so a full-scale unmodified run is not available as a gate.
## Recorded in ledger/infrastructure/2026-08-14-minimal-substitution-gate.cairn.md

Script: dyad_interaction_model.m
Profile: small
Knobs: N=3000; time_start_plot=0.5; time_end_plot=1.5
Requires: plot_len > lookahead_tolerance/dt; ~isempty(defn_objective_CIR);
 last_idx == round((time_end_plot+lookahead_tolerance)/dt)+1
Rationale: T = 3.0 keeps time_end_plot + lookahead_tolerance = 2.1 strictly
 below T, which selects the LOOKAHEAD branch of the last_idx conditional, the
 branch the published settings select. plot_len = 1601, so RE_metric is
 20 MB and the online-smoother triangles are 36 MB each. epsilon_resolution is
 left at its published 513: it is affordable at this size, and every knob left
 alone is one fewer difference to defend.

Script: dyad_interaction_model.m
Profile: tiny
Knobs: N=1200; time_start_plot=0.1; time_end_plot=0.8
Requires: plot_len > lookahead_tolerance/dt; ~isempty(defn_objective_CIR);
 last_idx == round(time_end_plot/dt)+1
Rationale: A second size, and deliberately the OTHER branch of the last_idx
 conditional: time_end_plot + lookahead_tolerance = 1.4 exceeds T = 1.2, so
 the truncating branch is taken. A parity that holds at one problem size only
 is a coincidence that has not been tested, and a conditional graded on one
 side only is half-graded.
 .
 The window is 0.7 rather than the 0.2 first tried. At 0.2 the window is
 shorter than the lookahead, and the reference's exact objective CIR indexes
 `subjective_CIR(:, 1:end-lookahead_tolerance/dt)`, which MATLAB evaluates to
 an EMPTY range rather than an error, so defn_objective_CIR came back 1x0
 and would have been "graded" against nothing. The Requires field above exists
 because of that, and is checked after every capture.

Script: noisy_predator_prey_model.m
Profile: predprey_dir1
Knobs: N=3000; time_start_plot=0; time_end_plot=6
Sections: 54-88, 93-114, 155-174, 213-256, 319-385, 472-473, 596-855
Requires: numel(f_x) == 1; plot_len > lookahead_tolerance/dt;
 ~isempty(defn_objective_CIR);
 last_idx == round((time_end_plot+lookahead_tolerance)/dt)+1
Rationale: Causal direction x(t) -> y: the prey y is OBSERVED, the predator x
 is unobserved.
 .
 This script must not be run top to bottom. It carries fourteen blocks headed
 "RUN THIS CODE SECTION TO STUDY THE CAUSAL RELATIONSHIP", seven per direction,
 and every one assigns the same names. `filter_mean` is written for direction
 one at line 225 and overwritten by direction two at line 276 before anything
 consumes it, so the direction-one smoother at line 321 reads direction TWO's
 filter, silently and with plausible numbers. The runner therefore composes
 only this direction's blocks, which is what the script's own instructions tell
 a user to do.
 .
 The first Requires condition is the discriminator: `f_x` is a SCALAR in
 direction one (line 97) and an ARRAY in direction two (line 126). If the
 runner ever picked up the wrong blocks, that assertion fails before anything
 is graded.
 .
 Lines 472-473 are carried in from the plotting block because the reporting
 window is defined there and the ACI block consumes it. The rest of the
 plotting is skipped: it is not needed, and a plotting failure would abort the
 runner before the ACI section.
 .
 lookahead_tolerance is 5 here, not the dyad's 0.6, and dt is 0.005, so the
 guard is 1000 steps. T = 15 and the window ends at 6, giving plot_len = 2201
 and keeping both the lookahead branch and a non-empty defn_objective_CIR.

Script: noisy_predator_prey_model.m
Profile: predprey_dir2
Knobs: N=3000; time_start_plot=0; time_end_plot=6
Sections: 54-88, 122-143, 182-201, 264-307, 393-458, 533-534, 863-1122
Requires: numel(f_y) == 1; plot_len > lookahead_tolerance/dt;
 ~isempty(defn_objective_CIR);
 last_idx == round((time_end_plot+lookahead_tolerance)/dt)+1
Rationale: Causal direction y(t) -> x: the predator x is OBSERVED, the prey y
 is unobserved. Mirror of predprey_dir1; see its rationale for why the script
 is composed rather than run. The discriminator is mirrored too, and `f_y` is
 the scalar in this direction (line 134).

Script: ENSO_model_cond_ACI_u_h_W_tau_unobs.m
Profile: enso_uhwtau
Knobs: T=100
Sections: 880-1340
Requires: size(filter_mean, 1) == 3; size(S_xoS_x_inv, 3) == N+1;
 all(isfinite(filter_mean(:))); all(isfinite(smoother_mean(:)))
Rationale: The configuration in which (T_C, T_E, I) are observed and
 (u, h_W, tau) are not. It is the one the committed enso fixture derives from.
 .
 Unlike the predator-prey script this one is linear: no "RUN THIS CODE SECTION"
 blocks, one filter_mean assignment, one smoother_mean. The section selection
 here is therefore for cost and dependency, not correctness. Lines 1-878 are
 the observational-data loader, which reads NetCDF and CSV from ENSO_DATA and
 is not needed to grade the recursions; lines 1341 onward are Hovmoeller
 plotting and the ACI analysis. What remains, 880-1340, is model setup, signal
 generation, monthly climatology, filter and smoother.
 .
 T is reduced from 15000 years to 100. At the published setting N = round(T/dt)
 is 3,000,000 and the filter allocates 3x3x(N+1) arrays (216 MB each, several
 of them). The recursions are O(N) and their correctness does not depend on the
 record length; the ACI analysis that does depend on it runs on N_s = 16800 and
 is outside the selected range.
 .
 The first Requires condition is the discriminator that this is the VECTOR path:
 filter_mean must have three rows. A scalar result would mean the wrong script
 or the wrong section had run.

Script: ENSO_model_cond_ACI_h_W_unobs.m
Profile: enso_h_W
Knobs: T=100
Sections: 883-1320
Requires: size(filter_mean, 1) == 1; all(isfinite(filter_mean));
 all(isfinite(smoother_mean))
Rationale: The ENSO configuration in which h_W alone is unobserved. Unlike the
 u_h_W_tau case this leaves a SCALAR latent state against a multi-dimensional
 observation, which is the discriminator asserted above. Linear script, no
 direction blocks; the selection drops only the observational-data loader
 before the setup banner and the plotting and ACI sections after the smoother.
 T is cut from 15000 years to 100, as for the other ENSO profile.

